  "final_message": "Done. The root cause was a float-precision comparison in `humanize.intword`:\n\n```python\nif not largest_ordinal and rounded_value * power == powers[ordinal + 1]:\n```\n\nFor values like `10**24 - 1`, the formatting rounds to `1000.0` sextillion, but `1000.0 * 10**21` evaluates to the float `1e24`, which is not `==` to the integer `10**24` — so the carry to the next named unit never happened, yielding `1000.0 sextillion`.\n\nFix (in `src/humanize/number.py`, `intword`):\n\n```python\nif (\n    not largest_ordinal\n    and rounded_value == 1000.0\n    and powers[ordinal] * 1000 == powers[ordinal + 1]\n):\n    # After rounding, we end up just at the next power. Only carry when the\n    # next named power is the immediate 1000x unit; this preserves the\n    # deliberate gap between decillion and googol.\n    ordinal += 1\n    rounded_value = 1.0\n```\n\nThis carries only when the rounded value hits `1000.0` and the next named power is the immediate 1000x step, avoiding float comparison entirely. Verified:\n\n- `999999 -> 1.0 million`, `999999999 -> 1.0 billion`, `10**24-1 -> 1.0 septillion`, down the chain to `10**33-1 -> 1.0 decillion`.\n- Gap preserved: `10**36-1 -> 1000.0 decillion` (no spurious carry into googol), while `10**100 -> 1.0 googol`.\n\nThe configured check `PYTHONPATH=src python -m pytest -q tests/test_number.py` passed (240 passed).",
